The '''2022 United States Senate elections''' will be held on November 8, 2022 (''[[Election Day (United States)|Election Day]]''), with 34 of the 100 seats in the [[United States Senate|Senate]] being contested in regular elections, the winners of which will serve six-year terms in the [[United States Congress]] from January 3, 2023, to January 3, 2029. Senators are divided into three groups, or [[Classes of United States senators|classes]], whose terms are staggered so that a different class is elected every two years. Class 3 senators, who were last elected in [[2016 United States Senate elections|2016]], will be up for election again in 2022.

All 34 Class 3 Senate seats are up for election in 2022; Class 3 currently consists of 14 [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] and 20 [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]]. Two special elections will also be held — in [[2022 United States Senate elections in California|California]], to fill the final weeks of [[Kamala Harris]]' term,<ref name=CA>{{Cite news|last=Wilson|first=Reid|date=September 28, 2021|title=California rule change means Padilla faces extra election|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/574242-california-rule-change-means-padilla-faces-extra-election|url-status=live|access-date=September 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210928225230/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/574242-california-rule-change-means-padilla-faces-extra-election|archive-date=September 28, 2021}}</ref> and in [[2022 United States Senate special election in Oklahoma|Oklahoma]], to serve the four remaining years of [[Jim Inhofe]]'s term.

Six Republican senators, [[Richard Shelby]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in Alabama|Alabama]]), [[Roy Blunt]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in Missouri|Missouri]]), [[Richard Burr]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in North Carolina|North Carolina]]), [[Rob Portman]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in Ohio|Ohio]]), [[Jim Inhofe]] ([[2022 United States Senate special election in Oklahoma|Oklahoma]]), [[Pat Toomey]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania]]), as well as one Democratic senator, [[Patrick Leahy]] ([[2022 United States Senate election in Vermont|Vermont]]), have announced that they are not seeking re-election; 15 Republicans and 13 Democrats are running for re-election.

Numerous other [[2022 United States elections|federal, state, and local elections]], including the [[2022 United States House of Representatives elections|2022 House elections]], will also be held on this date. The winners of these elections will serve beginning in the [[118th United States Congress]]. Democrats have held a majority in the Senate since January 20, 2021, following the party's twin victories in the run-offs for [[2020 United States Senate elections#Georgia|Georgia]]'s [[2020–21 United States Senate election in Georgia|regularly-scheduled]] and [[2020–21 United States Senate special election in Georgia|special]] 2020 Senate elections, and the inauguration of Democrat [[Kamala Harris]] as [[Vice President of the United States|vice president]]. There are 48 Democratic senators and two independent senators who caucus with them; with Harris' tie-breaking vote, the Democrats hold an effective 51-seat majority in the chamber.

This will be the first time in U.S. history in which multiple Senate races in the same year are contested between two [[African Americans|African-American]] nominees ([[2022 United States Senate election in Georgia|Georgia]] and [[2022 United States Senate election in South Carolina|South Carolina]]). Three previous elections{{efn|name="Senate elections"|They were in [[2004 United States Senate election in Illinois|Illinois in 2004]], and in [[2014 United States Senate special election in South Carolina|South Carolina in 2014]] and [[2016 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2016]].}} have taken place with two major-party African-American candidates.

