In contrast to [[2018 United States Senate elections|2018]], when Democrats were defending 10 seats in states that [[Donald Trump]] won in [[2016 United States presidential election|2016]], Democrats hold no seats in any state that was won by Trump in [[2020 United States presidential election|2020]]. Meanwhile, the Republicans are defending two seats ([[2022 United States Senate election in Wisconsin|Wisconsin]] and [[2022 United States Senate election in Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania]]) in states President [[Joe Biden]] won in 2020, compared to one seat ([[2018 United States Senate election in Nevada|Nevada]]) in a state that [[Hillary Clinton]] won in 2016 and was held by Republicans.

== Change in composition ==
