{{Main|2022 United States Senate election in Alabama}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Alabama|2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Alabama}}
Six-term Republican [[Richard Shelby]] was [[2016 United States Senate election in Alabama|re-elected in 2016]] with 64% of the vote. On February 8, 2021, Shelby announced that he would not seek re-election to a seventh term.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hulse |first1=Carl |title=Shelby, Veteran Senator from Alabama, Won't Seek Seventh Term |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/08/us/senator-richard-shelby-retire.html |access-date=February 8, 2021 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=February 8, 2021}}</ref>

[[Katie Britt]], Shelby's former Chief of Staff,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.alreporter.com/2021/06/08/katie-britt-announces-u-s-senate-candidacy/|title = Katie Britt announces U.S. Senate candidacy|date = June 8, 2021|author=Walker, Charlie|work=Alabama Political Reporter}}</ref> defeated six-term [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] [[Mo Brooks]] in the runoff Republican primary election. Britt and Brooks defeated businesswoman Karla DuPriest, former Army pilot and author [[Michael Durant]] and author Jake Schafer in the initial primary election.<ref>{{cite news |title=Qualifying for 2022 election ends Friday, Jan. 28 |url=https://www.andalusiastarnews.com/2022/01/25/qualifying-for-2022-election-ends-friday-jan-28/ |access-date=January 27, 2022 |publisher=The Andalusia Star News|author=Blankenship, Robert|date=January 25, 2022}}</ref>

[[Perennial candidate]] Will Boyd<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.alreporter.com/2022/01/31/will-boyd-announces-u-s-senate-run/|title=Will Boyd announces U.S. Senate run|last=Burkhalter|first=Eddie|work=Alabama Political Reporter|date=January 31, 2022|access-date=February 2, 2022}}</ref> defeated former [[Brighton, Alabama|Brighton]] mayor Brandaun Dean<ref>{{cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/120c52d4-a327-4608-b74e-31682af7ad0d/democratic-senate-candidate-says-that-he-will-be-the-%E2%80%9Cmost-anti-poverty-and-pro-b|title=Democratic Senate candidate says that he will be the "most anti-poverty and pro Black candidate"|last=Moseley|first=Brandon|work=1819 News|date=January 31, 2022|access-date=February 2, 2022}}</ref> and Lanny Jackson<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.al.com/news/2022/01/alabama-republican-candidates-far-outnumber-democrats-as-qualifying-for-may-24-primary-wraps-up.html|title=Alabama Republican candidates far outnumber Democrats as qualifying for May 24 primary wraps up|last=Cason|first=Mike|work=AL.com|date=January 28, 2022|access-date=February 2, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.al.com/news/2022/03/alabama-democrats-remove-trump-supporter-from-primary-ballot.html|title=Alabama Democrats remove Trump-supporter from primary ballot|last=Gattis|first=Paul|work=AL.com|date=March 4, 2022|access-date=March 5, 2022}}</ref> in the Democratic primary.

