{{Main|2022 United States Senate election in Alaska}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from Alaska|2022 United States House of Representatives election in Alaska}}
Three-term Republican [[Lisa Murkowski]] was [[2016 United States Senate election in Alaska|re-elected in 2016]] with 44.4% of the vote. Alaska adopted a top-four [[Jungle primaries|jungle primary]] system through the passage of a [[2020 Alaska Measure 2|ballot initiative]] in 2020, with the ultimate winner being decided via [[Ranked-choice voting in the United States|ranked-choice voting]]. Characterizations of the state as a "Safe" or "Solid" Republican stronghold may change if Murkowski decides to change her party affiliation to Independent as she suggested after the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack|Capitol attack]]. If she does so, she would most likely continue to caucus with Republicans in the Senate.<ref>{{cite tweet |author=Liz Ruskin |user=lruskin |number=1347800054099279872 |title=Lotta speculation about @lisamurkowski going Democrat. I asked: Are you considering joining the Democratic majority? Murkowski: "No. No. Absolutely, unequivocally not."}}</ref> On March 30, former [[Alaska Department of Administration]] commissioner Kelly Tshibaka announced that she was running against Murkowski following the state's GOP decision to censure her, with Tshibaka later receiving former President [[Donald Trump]]'s endorsement.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rogers |first1=Alex |title=Republican Kelly Tshibaka launches Senate campaign against Lisa Murkowski |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/29/politics/lisa-murkowski-gop-challenger-tshibaka/index.html |work=CNN |access-date=March 31, 2021|date=March 30, 2021}}</ref> Republican [[Governor of Alaska|Governor]] [[Mike Dunleavy (politician)|Mike Dunleavy]] has announced that he will not run, opting to [[2022 Alaska gubernatorial election|run for re-election]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Brooks|first=James|date=August 13, 2021|title=Alaska Gov. Mike Dunleavy will run for re-election in 2022|url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2021/08/13/alaska-gov-mike-dunleavy-files-paperwork-to-begin-fundraising-for-re-election-in-2022/|access-date=August 14, 2021|website=[[Anchorage Daily News]]|language=en-US}}</ref>

Murkowski and Tshibaka advanced to the general election, along with fellow Republican Buzz Kelley and leading Democratic candidate Pat Chesboro.<ref>{{cite news |title=Murkowski and Trump-backed Tshibaka advance to Alaska general election, NBC projects |work=[[CNBC]] |date=August 17, 2022 |access-date=September 15, 2022 |first=Kevin |last=Breuninger |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/08/17/murkowski-and-trump-backed-tshibaka-advance-to-alaska-general-election-nbc-projects.html}}</ref> Kelley suspended his campaign in September and endorsed Tshibaka, although he will still remain on the ballot.<ref>{{cite news |title=Alaska US Senate hopeful drops bid, backs fellow Republican |first=Mark |last=Thiessen |date=September 12, 2022 |access-date=September 15, 2022 |work=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-sarah-palin-donald-trump-general-campaigns-8b1b8dc64c4b2022e63f39e3fd359249}}</ref>
