Veteran and senior fellow at the Center for International Policy, Mathew Hoh is running for senate with the [[Green Party of the United States|Green Party]].<ref name=NC2022/>

[[Lara Trump]], daughter-in-law of former President [[Donald Trump]], and [[Lieutenant Governor of North Carolina|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Mark Robinson (American politician)|Mark Robinson]] briefly considered running for U.S. Senate, but both decided not to run.<ref name="LTrump1">{{cite news |last=Karni |first=Annie |date=November 19, 2020 |title=Will Lara Trump Be the Next Trump on a Ballot? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/19/us/politics/lara-trump.html |access-date=November 19, 2020 |website=The New York Times}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Choi |first1=Matthew |last2=Isenstadt |first2=Alex |last3=Arkin |first3=James |date=November 19, 2020 |title=Lara Trump considers run for Senate in North Carolina |work=[[Politico]] |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2020/11/19/lara-trump-north-carolina-senate-seat-438447 |access-date=November 22, 2020}}</ref><ref name="auto17"/><ref>{{cite web |title=NC Lt. Governor Mark Robinson Won't Run for Senate In 2022 |date=April 20, 2021 |url=https://www.wunc.org/politics/2021-04-20/north-carolina-lt-gov-mark-robinson-wont-run-senate-2022 |publisher=WUNC |access-date=April 20, 2021}}</ref>

[[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] [[Ted Budd]], who was endorsed by former President [[Donald Trump]], easily defeated former [[Governor of North Carolina| Governor]] [[Pat McCrory]] in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite news|last=Murphy|first=Brian|author2=Théoden Janes|date=April 14, 2021|url=https://www.charlotteobserver.com/news/politics-government/article250602039.html|title=Former North Carolina Gov. Pat McCrory announces bid for US Senate seat in 2022|work=The Charlotte Observer}}</ref><ref name="auto12">{{cite web|title= Ted Budd launches Senate bid in North Carolina|url= https://www.politico.com/news/2021/04/28/ted-budd-senate-race-north-carolina-484868|access-date=April 28, 2021|website=Politico|date=April 28, 2021|author=Arkin, James}}</ref><ref name="auto17">{{cite web|last=Polus|first=Sarah|date=June 5, 2021|title=Lara Trump on Senate bid: 'No for now, not no forever'|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/news/557022-lara-trump-on-senate-bid-no-for-now-not-no-forever|access-date=June 6, 2021|website=The Hill|language=en}}</ref>

Former [[Chief Justice]] of the [[North Carolina Supreme Court|state Supreme Court]] [[Cheri Beasley]]<ref name="axelrod1">{{cite web|last=Axelrod|first=Tal|title=Former North Carolina chief justice launches Senate campaign|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/550513-former-north-carolina-chief-justice-launches-senate-campaign|access-date=April 27, 2021|website=The Hill|date=April 27, 2021}}</ref> easily defeated [[Beaufort, North Carolina|Beaufort]] Mayor Rett Newton<ref>{{cite web |last1=Murphey |first1=Brian |title=Rett Newton: Beaufort mayor joins NC's US Senate race |url=https://www.newsobserver.com/news/politics-government/article250463631.html |website=The News & Observer |access-date=April 7, 2021}}</ref> in the Democratic primary.<ref>{{cite web |last=Murphy |first=Brian |title=Could Lara Trump run in NC in 2022? Open Senate seat expected to draw a GOP crowd |url=https://www.newsobserver.com/news/politics-government/article247295799.html |access-date=November 20, 2020 |website=The News & Observer}}</ref>

